## 2. Roadways: The Network That Reaches Everywhere

| Class | Managed by | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| National Highways | Central government (NHAI) | NH-44, Srinagar to Kanyakumari, India's longest |
| State Highways | State governments | Links state capitals with district towns |
| District and rural roads | District boards and panchayats | PMGSY village roads |
| Border roads | Border Roads Organisation (since 1960) | Strategic frontier roads in mountains |

- The **Golden Quadrilateral** links Delhi, Mumbai, Chennai, and
  Kolkata in a roughly 5,800 km loop; the North-South corridor
  (Srinagar-Kanyakumari) and East-West corridor (Porbandar-Silchar)
  complete the national spine.
- Numbering rule: odd-numbered highways run north-south and increase
  from east to west, while even-numbered ones run east-west and
  increase from north to south.

[tip]
On the Golden Quadrilateral, Delhi links directly with Mumbai and
Kolkata, while Chennai joins Mumbai and Kolkata — draw the diamond
once and every NH can be placed on it.
[/tip]

## 3. Railways: The Nation's Long Steel Thread

- The first train ran from Bombay (Bori Bunder) to Thane on 16 April
  1853 over 34 km; the network has since grown to roughly 68,000
  route km, among the world's largest under single management.
- Gauges were once a patchwork — broad (1.676 m), metre (1.000 m),
  and narrow (0.762 m) — now being unified under Project Unigauge;
  operations are organised into 17 zones.
- The **Konkan Railway** threads the Western Ghats between Roha and
  Mangalore with tunnels and tall bridges; two Dedicated Freight
  Corridors (eastern and western) decongest passenger lines; Vande
  Bharat trains mark the semi-high-speed era.

## 4. Waterways and Ports: Cheapest for Bulk

Inland waterways carry heavy cargo at the lowest cost: NW-1 on the
Ganga (Prayagraj-Haldia, about 1,620 km), NW-2 on the Brahmaputra
(Sadiya-Dhubri, about 891 km), and NW-3, the West Coast Canal in
Kerala (Kollam-Kottapuram, about 205 km).

| Port | State | Claim to fame |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Deendayal (Kandla) | Gujarat | Tidal port; tops the major ports in cargo handled |
| Mumbai | Maharashtra | Largest natural harbour of India |
| Nhava Sheva (JNPT) | Maharashtra | Largest container port |
| Marmagao | Goa | Historic iron ore exports |
| Kochi | Kerala | Natural lagoon harbour |
| Chennai | Tamil Nadu | Oldest artificial port on the east coast |
| Visakhapatnam | Andhra Pradesh | Deepest port of India |
| Kolkata-Haldia | West Bengal | Riverine port system far up the Hooghly |

[important]
Ocean shipping carries roughly 95 percent of India's overseas trade
by volume, so every policy on ports is really a policy on foreign
trade.
[/important]

## 5. Airways and Pipelines

- India's first commercial flight (1911) carried mail from Allahabad
  to Naini; J. R. D. Tata founded Tata Airlines in 1932, which became
  Air India, was nationalised in 1953, and returned to the Tata group
  in 2022.
- Air travel is unbeatable for long distances, emergencies, and
  high-value cargo, with Delhi and Mumbai as the busiest hubs.
- **Pipelines** move crude oil, petroleum products, and natural gas
  at low running cost once built — the Hazira-Vijaipur-Jagdishpur gas
  pipeline and the Salaya-Mathura crude line are classics — though
  their routes cannot be changed after laying.

## 6. Communication: From Postcards to the Internet

- The postal network is among the world's largest; the six-digit
  **PIN code**, introduced in 1972, reads region, sub-region, sorting
  district, and finally the exact post office.
- Telecom links every corner through fixed lines, mobiles, and the
  internet, with optical fibre now reaching villages under BharatNet.
- Mass media — radio, television, newspapers, cinema — carry market
  prices, weather warnings, and government schemes to hundreds of
  millions of homes.

[info]
Radio remains the cheapest and most far-reaching mass medium, which
is why cyclone warnings are still broadcast in local languages hour
by hour when storms approach the coast.
[/info]

## 7. International Trade: What We Sell and Buy

| Major exports | Major imports |
| --- | --- |
| Petroleum products and engineering goods | Crude petroleum, the single largest item |
| Gems and jewellery, pharmaceuticals | Gold and precious stones |
| Textiles, apparel, and rice | Electronics, machinery, and coal |

- India runs a **negative balance of trade**: the value of goods
  imported exceeds the value of goods exported, though services
  exports and remittances partly offset the gap.
- Trade rides on the port and airport networks above, with container
  traffic concentrated at JNPT and other gateway ports.

[warning]
Do not confuse the **balance of trade** (goods only) with the
**balance of payments** (goods, services, investments, and transfers
together) — India can run a goods deficit while services still earn
surpluses.
[/warning]

## 8. Quick Revision

| Mode | Landmark | Key fact |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Roads | Golden Quadrilateral | Delhi-Mumbai-Chennai-Kolkata, about 5,800 km |
| Railways | Bombay to Thane, 1853 | Broad, metre, narrow gauges; 17 zones |
| Waterways | NW-1 on the Ganga | Cheapest for heavy, bulky cargo |
| Airways | Tata Airlines, 1932 | Best for speed, emergencies, light cargo |
| Pipelines | HVJ gas line | Liquids and gases only; fixed route |
| Post | PIN code, 1972 | Six digits from region to post office |
| Trade | Sea carries about 95 percent by volume | Crude petroleum tops the import bill |
